<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#old-nubian">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Old Nubian</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Old Nubian is the medieval written form of the Nubian language, used in the Christian kingdoms of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> from about the 8th to the 15th century. Written in an adapted Coptic alphabet, it survives in church texts, letters, legal documents and graffiti, many found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qasr-ibrim">Qasr Ibrim</a>. It was used alongside Greek and Coptic in Makuria and its successor Dotawo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/old-nubian.jpg" alt="Old Nubian" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parchment_page_of_a_book,_Liber_Institionis_Michaelis_Archangeli,_written_in_Old_Nubian._9th-10_century_CE._From_Qasr_Ibrahim,_Egypt._British_Museum._EA_71305.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Old Nubian, also called Old Nobiin, is the ancestor of modern Nobiin and closely related to Dongolawi and Kenzi. It was used throughout the kingdom of Makuria, including the eparchy of Nobatia, and appears to have become the main language of its civil and religious administration from the 10th or 11th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a> remained in wide use, especially in religious contexts, and Coptic dominated funerary inscriptions. Over time more Old Nubian appeared in secular and religious writing, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. The consecration documents found with the remains of Archbishop Timotheos suggest that Greek and Coptic were still in use in the late 14th century, when Arabic was also widespread.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Nearly all the texts are written in a slanted uncial form of the Coptic alphabet that originated at the White Monastery in Sohag. It added three letters, two of them taken from the Meroitic alphabet, which suggests that the script was developed as early as the 6th century, after the collapse of the Meroitic state, although the first written evidence dates to the 8th. Double consonants marked gemination, a stroke above the line marked certain vowels, and a high dot, a slash and a double slash served as punctuation. Modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobiin">Nobiin</a> is tonal, but if Old Nubian was, its tones were not written.</p><p>The language has no grammatical gender. Plural and case markers, postpositions and a single definite determiner attach to the whole noun phrase, and a nominative-accusative system of four structural cases marks the core arguments. The verb is the most complex part of the grammar, carrying valency, tense, mood, aspect, person and pluractionality through a series of suffixes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The surviving material amounts to more than a hundred pages of documents and inscriptions: homilies, prayers, hagiographies, psalms and lectionaries, as well as legal documents and letters. F. Ll. Griffith published The Nubian Texts of the Christian Period in 1913. Gerald M. Browne edited Griffith's Old Nubian Lectionary and the Old Nubian texts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qasr-ibrim">Qasr Ibrim</a>, and wrote an Old Nubian dictionary and a grammar. Vincent van Gerven Oei published A Reference Grammar of Old Nubian in 2021, and the same year saw Sawarda, the first modern Nubian typeface modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, designed by Hatim-Arbaab Eujayl for books teaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobiin">Nobiin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Old Nubian” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alodia, called Alwa in Arabic and Aroua in <b>Old Nubian</b>, rose from the ruins of the Kingdom of Kush.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The church used Greek and Coptic in the liturgy, while <b>Old Nubian</b>, written in an alphabet adapted from Coptic, became a language of religious texts, legal documents and letters.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Old Nubian</b>, written from the 8th century AD, may be the oldest well-recorded African language outside the Afroasiatic family.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nubians">Nubians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Dotawo was the <b>Old Nubian</b> name used in documents for the realm of Makuria.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dotawo">Kingdom of Dotawo</a></li></ul></section>
